    <vspace*|1fn><with|font-series|bold|math-font-series|bold|Resolution and
    new comparison review> <datoms|<macro|x|<repeat|<arg|x>|<with|font-series|medium|<with|font-size|1|<space|0.2fn>.<space|0.2fn>>>>>|<htab|5mm>>
    <no-break><pageref|auto-4><vspace|0.5fn>
  </table-of-contents>

  Reviewed the regular and large-field restoration lemmas and the upper/lower
  numerical inflation argument in Chapter 11. No chapter edits made.

  <section|Checks><label|checks>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The norm's cover really is connected (Chapter 4), so the dispersal
    argument can charge disjoint initial connected paths around separated
    cell neighborhoods. Its distance applies between cells, not between two
    marks inside one cell.

    <item>With at most n=2 marks per cell, changing marker weight costs at
    most H^(Un) for a one-cell activity. Multicell activities pay through the
    spare connected-support exponent. The stated condition
    q\<gtr\>=kappa+Un+2 is sufficient, uniformly in the number of cells.

    <item>The failed-cutoff expansion is exact in the square-zero marker
    algebra. A component with new failed factors must retain an old selected
    inventory. At most 2^n new factors per cell can be charged to an old
    activity intersecting that cell neighborhood. Thus the auxiliary e^g and
    H^(2^n t(X)) accounting is consistent and does not presume independence
    of flags.

    <item>For the upper numerical step, l=max(1,H^-3/(alpha z+H^-q)) gives
    the stated nonprincipal bound in both cases l=1 and l\<gtr\>1. The choice
    alpha\<gtr\>=8T gives the incidence margin, eventually. Choosing the
    reported G exponent small relative to the actual owner exponent can pay
    m=G^-2 while retaining positive inventory decay. This must be a
    pre-history reserve, as the text now says.

    <item>The lower incidence estimate is valid when its stated incoming
    attenuation is retained. Paying G/a_low\<less\>=H^-q is available for the
    actual polynomial attenuation and reserved exponential decay, not for an
    arbitrary function a_low merely satisfying 0\<less\>=a_low\<less\>=1.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Explicit quantitative premise requested><label|explicit-quantitative-premise-requested>

  The argument uses R_e and R_(e+1) as support charges without yet bounding
  their growth in H. Require them, C_*, L^4 T_e and the finite incidence
  constants to be H^o(1) on the comparison windows (or fixed with a uniform
  finite-stage entry payment). Otherwise neither the new-factor auxiliary sum
  <verbatim|2^n C_* R_(e+1) H^-7/8 \<less\>= H^-1/2/2> nor the principal
  remainder <verbatim|2^n R_e H^-27/32 \<less\>= H^-3/4> follows. A cover
  bound for the actual prescribed enlarged supports, followed by the
  finite-stage delay, supplies the relevant R bounds; a bare diameter or
  number of marks is insufficient. Sent this request to the author and
  parent.

  <section|Actual restart supplier><label|actual-restart-supplier>

  Martin <verbatim|restarted-rank-history.tex> contains more than the
  abstract recurrence: its <verbatim|restart-actual-closure> proof supplies
  inflated owner/neutral/additive bounds and its final
  <verbatim|restart-chart-family> proposition keeps one alphabet and the
  exact endpoint density. In particular the endpoint is re-expressed at the
  smaller threshold, not assumed to have that threshold already. The final
  budget is B_j=ceil(log^2 x_j)^2 and tau_j=x_j^-1/2 exp((loglog x_j)^(3/2)).
  These data must match the local retiming and adjacent actual paths of the
  monograph. The present numerical review alone does not discharge that
  same-data construction; source pointers were sent to the author for the
  remaining join.

  <section|Resolution and new comparison review><label|resolution-and-new-comparison-review>

  The author added the explicit connected-cover bound C(1+A)^4 for prescribed
  supports, the resulting R_f, and finite stage delays controlling R_f, C_*,
  L^4 T_f and incidence constants by H^epsilon_j. The forced lemma now states
  the required H^o(1) premises. Requested the minor clarification that this
  fixed-cover geometry applies at the middle/restoration levels, where the
  same-cell readers have contracted, not at the separated opening level.

  Reviewed the new common extraction and covariance/comparison sequence.
  Found and had corrected the near/far assignment: the complementary term
  must be (1-chi) times the near masked sum; the full insertion minus chi
  Fnear does not vanish on chi=1 because of distant regular terms. The
  normalized p_u coefficient estimate was also added before the Wick
  perturbation bound. Completed-inverse curvature covariance, its Maxwell
  singularity, the finite Fourier-cube error, alias box-factor difference
  transfer, closed actual coarse background, negative transported diagonal
  after Wick ordering, and finite-coordinate moment perturbation are
  algebraically consistent in this targeted review. The 3V+1 lower versus
  3V-3 upper link count is retained in normalization. This review does not
  independently rederive every general weighted transport estimate of Chapter
  5.
